2186045417] [17592186172920 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173166 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172921 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172921 #edb/ref 1061 32211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172921 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "980cb0f2-0635-5e7b-926e-f8cbd302bf7b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172921 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173152 #edb/ref 1064 "The general equation of a second order (quadratic) curve is an expression of the form \n\n$$\n\\alpha_{11}x_1^2+2\\alpha_{12}x_1x_2 + \\alpha_{22}x_2^2 + 2\\alpha_{13}x_1+ 2\\alpha_{23}x_2 +\\alpha_{33}=0,\n$$\n\nwhere  $x_1$ and $x_2$ are variables, and $\\alpha_{ij} \\in \\Bbb R$ for all $1\\leq i,j \\leq 3.$ \n\nFor example, \n\n$$\n3x_1^2-x_1x_2-5x_2^2+5x_2 - 1 = 0\n$$\n\nis a general quadratic curve." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173152 #edb/ref 1065 5004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173152 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2d9b3527-1999-5169-affa-96a37f99fb01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173152 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173153 #edb/ref 1064 "In general, the cross-term of a quadratic equation can be eliminated by an orthogonal change of variables. That is, one of the kind \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} =P \\mathbf{y} ,\n$$\n\n  where the columns of $P$ form an orthonormal  eigenbasis for the corresponding quadratic form's matrix.\n\nLet $\\mathcal C$ be a plane curve defined as \n\n$$\n\\mathcal C:\\: 2x_1x_2 -2\\sqrt2x_1+2\\sqrt2x_2 =8.\n$$\n\nHow do we determine the type of our curve?\n\nNotice that the quadratic form $2x_1x_2$ can be reduced to canonical form $y_1^2-y_2^2$ using the change of variables\n![](images/ab/ab1ecd406bcaf2f48f60d0e97c214f1fae9ab911b08ebe2bd74a6e43e0aa524b.svg)\n\nFirst, let's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n![](images/18/1820ce650da02daa432023803a773821f63753e2adda1c6e55964fdddb913934.svg)\n\nWe know that this change of variables reduces the corresponding quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf x) = 2x_1x_2$ to its canonical form. Indeed, performing the change of variables on $\\mathcal C,$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}+2\\sqrt{2}{x}_{2}=8\\,⟹\\,{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}+4{y}_{2}=8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n and, by completing the  square for the variable $y_2$,  we get the equation \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{2}-{({y}_{2}-2)}^{2} & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of the resulting equation in the $y_1,y_2$ variables is the following.\n\n![](images/d5/d532f5207a9d518f92024c86689285834b86c961d0f647d6ee99ad6dd9c7c329.png)\n\nNotice that we can write down the change-of-variables matrix as\n![](images/2d/2d128752c7b7a73db6a3dfc8c1fc232347b01d50376b46b08704a9ebd4e3ceb7.svg)\nwhich is a rotation matrix. Looking at the diagram above, we see that going from one coordinate system to another can be viewed as a rotation of our axes by $45^\\circ.$\n\n**Watch out!** In general, this action is an orthogonal transformation (it can be a rotation, a reflection, or a combination of such transformations).\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}={y}_{1}, \\\\ {z}_{2}={y}_{2}-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{2}-{({y}_{2}-2)}^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis (standard) equation represents a hyperbola in which  both semiaxes are equal to $2.$      \n\nIn the diagram below we represent the curve in the $z_1,z_2$ variables.\n\n![](images/c3/c36709f752bd9c319662e7e2150c54031cc8fb3934be37711c65e7a7668829ca.png)\n\nLooking at the diagram above, we see that in the final step we simply translated our system by $(0,2)$ in $y$-coordinates.\n\n**Note:** Finding the simplest equation of a quadratic curve can be viewed as the following series of steps:\n\n- **Step 1**. We eliminate the cross-term by reducing the quadratic part to canonical form. Geometrically, this is equivalent to an orthogonal transformation of our coordinate system.\n\n- **Step 2**. We eliminate the linear terms by completing the squares. Geometrically, this is equivalent to a translation of our coordinate system." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173153 #edb/ref 1065 5005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173153 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "cd5dde1f-7d5c-58d9-8674-6bf984a51d0d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173153 #edb/ref 1067 "Classifying a General Quadratic Curve" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173155 #edb/ref 1064 "A quadratic equation with no cross-terms can be rewritten in standard form by completing the square. Let's see how this is done in the following example. \n\nSuppose we are given the equation  \n\n$$\n2x_1^2+x_2^2 - 4x_1 + 4x_2 = 2,\n$$\n\nwhat type of curve does it represent?\n\nFirst, we complete the squares for the variables $x_1$ and $x_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}-4{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} & =2 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1})+({x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{2}) & =2 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}+1-1)+({x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{2}+4-4) & =2 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}-1)^{2}-2+({x}_{2}+2)^{2}-4 & =2 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}-1)^{2}+({x}_{2}+2)^{2} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}={x}_{1}-1, \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}+2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2} & =8 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{8} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{8})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents an ellipse with semiaxes $2$ and $\\sqrt{8}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173155 #edb/ref 1065 5015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173155 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "bd1f3565-37df-5db7-aa06-a573b6b58f8a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173155 #edb/ref 1067 "Classifying a Quadratic Curve That Does Not Contain a Cross-Product Term" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173165 #edb/ref 1064 "There are three basic so-called *non-degenerate quadratic curves*:\n\n| Standard Equation | Description |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=1$ | Ellipse |\n| $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=1$ | Hyperbola |\n| $x_2^2= 2px_1$ | Parabola |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173165 #edb/ref 1065 5129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173165 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "31519439-ac97-5940-b01b-de36561f9618" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173165 #edb/ref 1067 "Non-Degenerate Quadratic Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173166 #edb/ref 1064 "There are also a few so-called *degenerate (real) quadratic curves:*\n\n| Standard Equation | Description |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=0$ | Pair of\n intersecting  lines |\n| ${x_i^2}=a^2$ | Pair of \n parallel lines |\n| ${x_i^2}=0$ | Pair of \n coincident lines |\n| $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=0$ | Single point |\n\nLet's see why these equations define the described curves:\n\n- $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=0$ is equivalent to $(bx_1 - ax_2)(bx_1 + ax_2)=0$ which in turn gives equations of two lines $bx_1 - ax_2=0$ and $bx_1 + ax_2=0$ intersecting at the origin.\n\n- ${x_i^2}=a^2$ is equivalent to $(x_i - a)(x_i + a)=0$ which in turn gives equations of two parallel lines $x_i =a$ and $x_i=-a.$\n\n- ${x_i^2}=0$ gives equations of two coincident lines $x_i =0$ and $-x_i=0.$\n\n- $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=0$ is equivalent to $b^2x_1^2+a^2x_2^2=0$ which in turn gives a single point, the origin, since the sum of two squares is $0$ if and only if both squares are $0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173166 #edb/ref 1065 5130 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173166 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7fa5c07c-714f-555e-9253-e8c6b56484e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173166 #edb/ref 1067 "Degenerate (Real) Quadratic Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173167 #edb/ref 1064 "Finally, some quadratic equations might define an *empty set* (or so-called *imaginary curve*):\n\n| Standard Equation | Description |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=-1$ | Imaginary ellipse |\n| $x_i^2=-a^2$ | Pair of imaginary\n parallel lines |\n\nLet's see why these equations define the described curves:\n\n- Although there are no real solutions, the equation $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2} = -1,$ which is equivalent to $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{(a\\textrm{i})^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{(b\\textrm{i})^2} = 1,$ has imaginary solutions, so we could interpret this as an imaginary ellipse.\n\n- Although there are no real solutions, the equation ${x_i^2}=-a^2$ is equivalent to $(x_i - a\\textrm{i})(x_i + a\\textrm{i})=0$ which in turn gives equations of two imaginary parallel lines $x_i =a\\textrm{i}$ and $x_i=-a\\textrm{i}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173167 #edb/ref 1065 5131 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173167 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "81eb37e3-202d-5934-93ce-8534c3ffc1a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173167 #edb/ref 1067 "Empty Sets" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366629 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186366630 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366629 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95397" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366629 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366629 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+4\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}=4.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $2{y}_{1}^{2}$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186366629 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3c25a282-f09e-5080-ae4d-6a92631e6519" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366629 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}+4\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}=4\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} &=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+4\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1} & =4 \\\\ 2{y}_{1}^{2}+4\\sqrt{2}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2}) & =4 \\\\ 2{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{1}-4{y}_{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{1}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{1}-4{y}_{2} & =4 \\\\ 2({y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{1})-4{y}_{2} & =4 \\\\ 2({y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{1}+1-1)-4{y}_{2} & =4 \\\\ 2({y}_{1}+1)^{2}-2\\cdot 1-4{y}_{2} & =4 \\\\ 2({y}_{1}+1)^{2} & =4{y}_{2}+6 \\\\ 2({y}_{1}+1)^{2} & =4({y}_{2}+\\frac{3}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1} &={y}_{1}+1, \\\\ {z}_{2} &={y}_{2}+\\frac{3}{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({y}_{1}+1)^{2} & =4({y}_{2}+\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\ 2{z}_{1}^{2} & =4{z}_{2} \\\\ {z}_{1}^{2} & =2{z}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents a parabola that opens in the direction of the positive ${z}_{2}$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186366630 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366630 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366632 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366630 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366630 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366630 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366630 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366630 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366630 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186366633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366630 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "23070fd2-4a47-5c26-ae55-52058929b2df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366631 #edb/ref 1080 "A pair of intersecting lines" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366631 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366631 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b13d7123-12c3-5fba-865d-79d04d134405" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366632 #edb/ref 1080 "An empty set" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366632 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366632 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9eb75fec-aad0-52b8-8f0b-256603947924" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366633 #edb/ref 1080 "A parabola" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366633 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366633 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "335c53ad-9b06-5ae9-9d82-ab45fdedd159" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366634 #edb/ref 1080 "A pair of parallel lines" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366634 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366634 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "50865aa7-b849-5510-a201-43adab487ed6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366635 #edb/ref 1080 "A hyperbola with semiaxes  $3$ and $\\sqrt{2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366635 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366635 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "30cf17d6-9da7-5a11-b299-70e72069e0e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366732 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186366733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366732 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96075" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366732 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366732 #edb/ref 1071 "What type of quadratic curve is defined by the following equation?\n\n$$\n3{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}^{2}=\\frac{35}{3}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366732 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bb83c793-f479-5cb9-b03e-05eb6c2cfac2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366732 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we complete the squares for the variable ${x}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{35}{3} \\\\ 3({x}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{1})+4{x}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{35}{3} \\\\ 3({x}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{1}+{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2})+4{x}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{35}{3} \\\\ 3{({x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-3{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{35}{3} \\\\ 3{({x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}+4{x}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{35}{3} \\\\ 3{({x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{36}{3} \\\\ 3{({x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2} & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &={x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ {y}_{2} &={x}_{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{2}^{2} & =12 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents an ellipse with semiaxes $2$ and $\\sqrt{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186366733 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366733 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366733 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366736 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366733 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366733 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] 